        <p>The joint return wins on two lines at once. The standard deduction doubles to $32,200, and the brackets widen: 10% runs to $24,800 of taxable income and 12% runs to $100,800, so none of this $86,360 year reaches the 22% bracket at all. That drops the marginal rate from 22% to 12% and the effective federal rate from 14.6% to 8.3%. FICA does not change, because it is charged on gross pay with no deduction in front of it. The gap is $615.50 a month, or $3.56 on every hour worked. Two knock-on effects are worth knowing. A 5% 401(k) contribution of $5,928 a year saves only $711.36 of federal tax at the 12% marginal rate, so it costs $5,216.64 of take-home, which is $592.80 more than the same contribution costs a single filer. And $10,000 of spending money takes 208.79 take-home hours (5.22 weeks) instead of 225.51. One comparison still favors the salary: a $120,000 salary filed jointly keeps $100,780, which is $1,157.04 ahead of this hourly year, so the salary wins the joint-filing test by a little more than it wins the single test. Our <a href="/articles/how-to-calculate-taxes-on-paycheck/">paycheck-tax guide</a> shows how to read the filing-status line on your own stub.</p>
