        <p>The kept amounts sit within $39 of each other, because all three paths push about $1,750 of the year into the 24% bracket, so the tax bite is nearly the same either way. The real difference is the hours. The raise is the only path that pays for zero extra hours, and it keeps paying every year after, which is why it wins. Overtime hours are the best-paid hours on the table at $59.56 kept each, but they only exist if the work is there. Straight extra hours are the worst-paid at $39.70 kept each, and they cost the most time. So if you can only pick one lever, pick the raise. If the raise is not on the table, overtime beats extra straight hours by about $20 an hour kept. Our <a href="/articles/how-much-is-a-5-percent-raise/">5%-raise guide</a> prices what a percentage raise does at this rate, and the <a href="/overtime-calculator/">overtime calculator</a> prices any hours pattern you enter.</p>
