        <p>The bracket detail comes first because most guides skip it: $52,060 of the $102,460 taxable income sits in the 22% bracket, and that slice costs $11,453.20 in Step 2. Tax the whole year at a flat 22% and the federal bill comes out $5,288.00 too high. The raise section below puts a price on what that slice does to your next $1. The take-home figure itself has limits. It is federal tax only, for a single filer taking the $16,100 standard deduction, with no state tax, no 401(k), and no health premium in it. It also counts 40 paid hours in all 52 weeks, which no real year delivers. The sections that follow run the same $57.00 rate through every schedule, every overtime pattern, and every unpaid-week gap, so you can find the version that matches your year.</p>
