        <p>Read the monthly row first, because it is the one that trips people up. It divides by 12, not by 4. Four weeks is only $9,120.00, and the $760.00 gap is the extra 1.33 weeks the average month really holds. Then the biweekly row. Biweekly and semi-monthly are not the same check: biweekly is 26 paychecks of $4,560.00, semi-monthly is 24 paychecks of $4,940.00, and the two extra biweekly checks a year are what make the totals equal. A month with three biweekly checks pays $13,680.00 gross ($10,642.73 take-home). A normal two-check month pays $9,120.00 ($7,095.15 take-home). That swing is why biweekly pay feels lumpy. Our <a href="/articles/how-many-paychecks-in-a-year/">paychecks-per-year guide</a> maps the three-paycheck months for every 2026 cycle, and our <a href="/articles/biweekly-vs-semi-monthly-pay-difference/">biweekly-versus-semimonthly comparison</a> runs the two schedules through a full year on the same salary.</p>
