        <p>Read the last column from the top row down and the shape of the year shows itself: fewer hours means more kept from each hour. Twenty hours a week keeps $47.90 an hour. Forty hours keeps $44.34. The gap is $3.56 an hour on the same $57.00 rate, and the bracket is behind it. A 20-hour year carries $43,180.00 of taxable income, and none of it reaches the 22% bracket. A 25-hour year carries $58,000.00, and $7,600.00 of it lands in the 22% bracket, so the 25-hour row is the first schedule on this page where the 22% bracket turns up at all. Three rows deserve a pause. First, a 35-hour week clears $100,000 gross on its own, at $103,740.00, and its $81,811.09 of take-home already beats a $100,000 salary by $2,631.09, with no overtime and no raise. Second, the 37.5-hour paid week, the 7.5-hour office day, pays $111,150.00 gross, still ahead of a $110,000 salary by $1,150.00 gross and $809.02 of take-home. Give back 2.5 hours a week and you give up $7,410.00 gross and $5,212.94 of take-home. Third, the move from 30 to 40 hours is worth $29,640.00 gross and $20,851.74 of take-home ($1,737.65 a month). It is the single biggest lever on this page, bigger than any raise in the nearby-rates table. Part-time keeps the bigger share as well: at 20 hours you keep 84.0% of gross, against 77.8% full-time, and a 25-hour week at $57 beats a $70,000 salary's take-home by $2,884.35, while a 20-hour week beats a full-time $27-an-hour job by $2,506.92. Our <a href="/articles/how-to-convert-part-time-hourly-pay-to-annual-salary/">part-time conversion guide</a> works the same ladder for any rate and any hours, and our <a href="/articles/how-many-hours-is-full-time/">full-time-hours guide</a> explains why 30 hours is the line where benefits usually start.</p>
