        <p>The $120,000 row is the closest call on the whole page: the salary leads by only $1,440.00 on gross and $1,013.04 on take-home, which is why the overtime section prices how little it takes to flip it. The ladder is flat here: the $56-to-$57 step and the $57-to-$58 step both keep exactly $1,463.28, and the next section prices why the ladder stays flat from here on. When you compare, read the take-home column, not the gross column. Against salaries, $57 an hour beats a $115,000 salary by $3,560.00 gross and $2,504.46 of take-home, more than double the $1,480.00 gross and $1,041.18 of take-home that $56 managed against the same salary. The $60,000 salary is the clearest win for the hourly job: $57 an hour beats it by $58,560.00 gross and $41,846.96 of take-home, and no realistic benefits package closes a gap that size. Our <a href="/articles/hourly-vs-salary-which-pays-more/">hourly-versus-salary comparison</a> prices any two offers head to head on the same paid-weeks basis, and our <a href="/calculators/salary-comparison/">salary-comparison calculator</a> runs any two offers through the same paid-weeks math.</p>
