        <p class="source-note"><strong>Sources and method:</strong> Federal figures use the 2026 tax brackets, the $16,100 standard deduction for single filers ($32,200 filing jointly), and the 7.65% FICA rate (6.2% Social Security up to the $184,500 wage base, 1.45% Medicare), from IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-32. The 2,080-hour year is the standard 40-hour-week convention, and monthly pay is gross divided by 12, not by 4. Three independent checks back the headline: DollarCalcs quotes the same $118,560 gross and $92,237 take-home but labels 22.2% an effective federal rate when it is really the combined federal-plus-FICA rate, takehomepay.online uses a federal figure of $17,992.90 from mixed 2026/2027 brackets, which is $739.70 too high, and folds California state tax into its headline take-home of $84,383.66, and TimeHackHero still runs on 2023 brackets with a flat 24% rate and lands at $81,032.21. This article is general information, not tax or legal advice.</p>
